{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T09:40:40.308922", "data": {"url": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Gold_price_in_India", "title": "Gold price in India \u2014 Grokipedia", "content": "Fact-checked by Grok 4 months ago Gold price in India Ara Eve Leo Sal 1x The gold price in India refers to the domestic market rate for 24-karat gold , most commonly quoted per 10 grams , serving as the primary benchmark for jewellery purchases, personal investments, and cultural transactions across the country. These prices are highly dynamic, influenced by global spot prices, the INR-USD exchange rate , import duties, inflation , international market factors, and local demand. [1] Gold holds significant cultural and economic importance in India, where it is widely regarded as a safe investment and a perfect hedge against inflation. High demand during festivals , weddings , and other occasions often contributes to price fluctuations, while daily rates are sourced from reputed jewellers across major cities. Prices vary by location due to local taxes and market conditions, with 24-karat gold typically serving as the standard for pure gold valuation. [1] As of March 8, 2026, the indicative gold price in India is \u20b916,364 per gram for 24-karat gold (approximately \u20b9163,640 per 10 grams or \u20b9190,800 per tola, where 1 tola \u2248 11.66 grams) and \u20b915,000 per gram for 22-karat gold (approximately \u20b9174,900 per tola). These are indicative rates excluding GST, TCS, and making charges. Prices vary by city, jeweler, taxes, and market conditions (e.g., MCX vs physical retail). Rates remain subject to rapid change and should be verified with live sources. [1] [2] [3] [1] [4] [5] The market also includes rates for 22-karat and 18-karat gold, commonly used in jewellery , alongside tools for calculating prices based on weight and purity. [1] The gold market in India is shaped by both global economic trends and domestic factors, making it a key indicator of investor sentiment and economic stability . [1] Current gold prices Spot and retail rates In India, gold prices are most commonly quoted for 24-karat purity per 10 grams, which serves as the standard unit for retail transactions, jewellery purchases, and physical investment in bars or coins . As of 8 March 2026, the gold rate in India for 24-carat gold is approximately \u20b9163,640 per 10 grams (\u20b916,364 per gram national average/Mumbai rate), and for 22-carat gold around \u20b9150,000 per 10 grams (\u20b915,000 per gram). These indicative rates reflect market conditions influenced by global and domestic factors. Consumers should verify live rates from reliable sources due to fluctuations. [1] [4] [6] As of 8 March 2026, the price of 10 grams of 18-karat gold in India is approximately \u20b9122,730 (\u20b912,273 per gram) in major cities like Mumbai, Bangalore, Hyderabad, and Kolkata. These are base market rates excluding making charges or taxes. Consumers should verify live rates from reliable sources due to fluctuations. [1] For lower purity gold such as 9-karat (37.5% pure), prices are calculated proportionally based on the 24-karat rate, as 9K gold is not directly listed on major sites like Goodreturns. As of 8 March 2026, this approximates \u20b96,137 per gram (0.375 \u00d7 \u20b916,364). Local retail rates are typically higher than international spot conversions due to taxes and premiums. Consumers should verify live rates from reliable sources due to fluctuations. [1] Gold prices are also quoted in traditional units such as 1 tola (approximately 11.66 grams). As of 8 March 2026, the price of 1 tola of 24-karat gold in India is approximately \u20b9190,800, corresponding to per-gram rates of \u20b916,364, with variations by city, time of day, source, and market conditions. For 22-karat gold, 1 tola is approximately \u20b9174,900. Consumers should verify live rates from reliable sources due to fluctuations. [1] Additionally, as of 8 March 2026, the MCX gold futures price reflects current market dynamics. Note that futures prices can differ from physical spot and retail rates due to contract specifications, expiry expectations, and trading dynamics. Consumers and investors should verify live rates from reliable sources due to the highly dynamic nature of these markets. [5] The spot price refers to the base market price of pure gold, closely aligned with the international spot price converted to Indian rupees (after accounting for currency exchange ), while the retail price incorporates additional domestic costs such as import duties, goods and services tax (GST at 3%), and minor local premiums charged by jewellers or traders. Daily and weekly price movements are common, driven primarily by changes in global spot prices and INR-USD exchange rates, with intraday variations often observed in response to economic news, geopolitical events , or trading activity in international markets . Live rates are widely available from financial portals, jewellers' associations , and commodity exchanges , allowing consumers to check real-time updates before purchases. Prices are generally uniform at the national level for the standard 10-gram unit, with minor adjustments reflecting local market conditions, jeweler-specific premiums, and differences between MCX futures and physical retail markets. Consumers should verify live rates from reliable sources before making purchases due to the highly dynamic nature of prices. City-wise variations Gold prices in India display minor variations across major cities, typically amounting to a few hundred rupees per 10 grams for 24-karat gold, despite the national baseline being influenced by global spot prices and import costs. As of 8 March 2026, national rates for 24-carat gold are approximately \u20b9163,640 per 10 grams (\u20b916,364 per gram in Mumbai), with minor variations across cities reflecting local premiums. Similar minor variations apply to 22-carat and 18-carat rates relative to their national averages. These are base market rates excluding making charges or taxes. [1] [7] These differences arise primarily from local factors such as jeweller margins, transportation and logistics costs , regional demand patterns, and competition among sellers. In cities like Delhi and Mumbai , prices are often slightly higher or aligned with the national average due to higher operational costs and consistent demand from investors and buyers. Southern cities like Chennai and Bangalore may show variations due to intense competition among jewellers and high volume sales during festival and wedding seasons. Kolkata and Hyderabad prices generally align closely with the national average, with variations typically small relative to the overall price level. Rates can vary slightly by jeweler, time of day, and source (e.g., MCX vs. physical retail). [1] [7] These city-wise differences are relatively small compared to the overall price level and are monitored daily by local jewellers' associations and financial portals for retail accuracy. [1] Consumers should verify live rates from reliable sources due to fluctuations. Comparison with international benchmarks The gold price in India is typically higher than major international benchmarks, such as the London Bullion Market Association (LBMA) Gold Price and COMEX futures prices, due to import duties, goods and services tax (GST), and other local costs. The difference is expressed as a premium (or occasionally discount) over the international spot price converted to INR using the prevailing exchange rate. This premium incorporates customs duty on gold imports (currently 6% as per policy since 2024), 3% GST, and additional local charges or demand-supply dynamics. As of 8 March 2026, the Indian price for 24-karat gold per 10 grams carries a premium over the converted international spot price, typical due to import duties, taxes, and local market factors. Consumers should verify live rates from reliable sources due to fluctuations. [1] The Indian price for 24-karat gold per 10 grams has typically carried a premium over the international benchmark , with variations based on import policy and market conditions. [1] Historically, the premium has ranged from near zero or slight discounts during periods of high export or low demand to over 20% in times of restricted imports or peak seasonal buying, though the typical range in recent years has been 5-15%. This premium makes Indian gold more expensive than global equivalents, influencing jewellery demand and investment decisions. Global spot prices, which form the base for these comparisons, are determined by international factors. Historical trends Long-term price evolution The long-term price evolution of gold in India has been characterized by a strong upward trajectory in nominal terms over the past five decades, with prices rising from a few hundred rupees per 10 grams in the 1970s to over \u20b91,50,000 per 10 grams in the mid-2020s (as of 2026). In the 1970s, nominal prices for 24-karat gold per 10 grams were in the low hundreds, such as \u20b9184 in 1970 and \u20b9937 in 1979, reflecting relatively stable global markets and limited domestic liberalisation . By the 1980s, prices began accelerating, reaching several thousand rupees per 10 grams, from \u20b91,330 in 1980 to \u20b93,140 in 1989, amid global price surges and rupee devaluation . The 1990s saw further increases to the range of \u20b93,200\u2013\u20b94,234 per 10 grams, supported by economic reforms and growing demand. The 2000s marked a period of rapid appreciation, with prices climbing to around \u20b914,500 per 10 grams by 2009, driven by strong global bull markets. The 2010s continued the upward momentum, with prices crossing \u20b930,000 per 10 grams in the early part of the decade and reaching highs around \u20b935,000 by the late 2010s (e.g., \u20b935,220 average in 2019). The 2020s have seen substantial increases, with nominal prices exceeding \u20b91,50,000 per 10 grams as of 2026 (e.g., around \u20b91,57,400\u2013\u20b91,83,000 depending on source and city), influenced by post-pandemic economic recovery and geopolitical factors . Overall, the compound annual growth rate in nominal terms has been substantial over these decades, though exact rates vary by period. When adjusted for inflation, real (inflation-adjusted) gold prices in India have shown more moderated growth, with periods of positive real returns during high-inflation eras and occasional declines during low-inflation or strong rupee phases. Gold has generally served as a hedge against inflation over the long term, though real returns have lagged behind nominal gains due to persistent rupee depreciation against the US dollar . Key turning points include the post-1971 period following the end of the Bretton Woods system and the liberalization era of the 1990s , which amplified domestic price sensitivity to global movements. [8] [1] Major periods of volatility Gold prices in India have experienced several notable periods of sharp volatility, often aligned with global economic shocks and domestic factors. During the 2008 global financial crisis , gold prices in India surged as a safe-haven asset, rising from approximately \u20b99,800 per 10 grams in early 2008 to over \u20b915,500 by year-end, representing a roughly 58% increase. This upward volatility boosted investment demand while reducing jewellery purchases due to elevated costs. In 2013, the US Federal Reserve 's taper tantrum triggered a sharp decline in global gold prices, which was reflected in India with prices falling from around \u20b931,000 per 10 grams to about \u20b925,000, a drop of approximately 20%. The correction led to a temporary slump in jewellery demand and increased investor selling. The COVID-19 pandemic in 2020 caused one of the most pronounced upward movements, with prices climbing from around \u20b938,000 per 10 grams in January to over \u20b956,000 in August, a rise of nearly 50%. This surge was driven by global uncertainty and low interest rates, resulting in strong investment inflows but significantly reduced jewellery consumption as prices hit record levels. Following the Russia-Ukraine conflict in 2022 , gold prices exhibited heightened volatility, rising sharply from around \u20b948,000 per 10 grams to over \u20b960,000 amid geopolitical tensions and inflation fears. This period saw fluctuating demand patterns, with increased investment buying offsetting some decline in ornamental purchases. In March 2025, 22-carat gold prices in India showed short-term volatility, ranging from approximately \u20b979,400 per 10 grams in early March to \u20b984,250 per 10 grams by the end of the month, with typical prices around \u20b980,000\u201384,000 per 10 grams. Kerala market data, which closely aligns with national trends, indicated a general upward trend throughout the month amid prevailing market factors. [9] [10] On February 1, 2026, gold and silver futures on the Multi Commodity Exchange (MCX) plunged sharply, declining up to 9% and hitting lower circuit limits ahead of the Union Budget 2026-27 presentation. Gold April futures fell to approximately \u20b91,39,000 per 10 grams, while silver March futures dropped to around \u20b92.65 lakh per kg, amid profit booking after recent record highs, a firmer US dollar, global market volatility, and cautious investor sentiment regarding potential budget policy changes. This downward correction highlighted the market's responsiveness to domestic fiscal events and extended the profit-taking trend from preceding sessions. [11] [12] In early 2026, the escalation of Iran-US tensions into conflict boosted gold and silver prices in India as investors sought safe-haven assets, with gold jumping by around Rs 6,000 per 10 grams and silver by Rs 10,000 per kg amid fears of regional instability and disrupted energy supplies. Similar upward pressure was seen in late 2025 amid building tensions. [13] These episodes highlight how global events can cause rapid price swings in the Indian market, often leading to shifts between investment and jewellery demand. Gold price outlook Gold price forecasts for 2026 and beyond are generally bullish according to several major financial institutions, with predictions for the end of 2026 ranging approximately from US$5,645 to $6,700 per ounce (with some upside potential to $7,200 in risk scenarios such as escalated geopolitical tensions). Specific forecasts include Ventura Securities at $5,645, Deutsche Bank at $6,000, UBS at $5,900\u2013$6,200, J.P. Morgan at $6,300, and GlobalData at $6,100\u2013$6,700 (with potential to $7,000 in risk-off scenarios). These projections are primarily driven by sustained central bank gold purchases, strong investor demand for safe-haven assets, persistent geopolitical tensions, and ongoing reserve diversification trends away from traditional currencies. [14] [15] [16] [17] [18] In India, gold prices are expected to trend upward in line with global movements, influenced by rupee depreciation against the US dollar, inflation, and seasonal demand factors such as festivals and weddings. As of February 2026, prices are around \u20b91.53 lakh per 10 grams, with forecasts indicating an upward trend throughout the year, ranging from \u20b91.5 lakh to \u20b91.95 lakh per 10 grams. GlobalData forecasts \u20b91.75 lakh to \u20b91.95 lakh by end-2026, while other analyses suggest \u20b91.5 lakh to \u20b91.75 lakh, driven by geopolitical tensions, safe-haven demand, and currency factors. [18] These are analyst predictions and subject to significant uncertainty, potentially changing with evolving economic conditions, monetary policies, and geopolitical events. They do not constitute guarantees of future performance. Factors influencing prices Global market drivers The global spot price of gold, which forms the foundation for domestic prices in India, is primarily driven by international macroeconomic , geopolitical , and supply-demand dynamics. Gold is widely regarded as a safe-haven asset, with prices rising during periods of geopolitical uncertainty, such as conflicts or trade tensions, as investors seek protection from market volatility. Strength of the US dollar plays a significant inverse role, since gold is denominated in dollars; a stronger dollar typically depresses gold prices by making it more expensive for holders of other currencies, while a weaker dollar supports higher prices. Inflation expectations also influence demand, as gold serves as a hedge against rising prices and currency devaluation , particularly when real interest rates are low or negative. Central bank activity has become a major driver in recent years, with many emerging market central banks accumulating gold reserves as a diversification strategy away from US dollar assets. Net purchases reached record levels in 2022 and continued strongly in 2023, providing sustained support to prices even amid higher interest rates. [19] On the supply side, mine production remains relatively stable, with annual output hovering around 3,000\u20133,300 tonnes in recent years, while recycling supply fluctuates based on price levels and economic conditions, adding flexibility to total supply. These global factors collectively determine the international benchmark price , which Indian prices track closely, though with local adjustments. Domestic economic and demand factors Domestic demand plays a pivotal role in driving gold prices in India , with consumption heavily tilted towards jewellery and influenced by seasonal patterns, economic conditions, and regional differences. Seasonal demand peaks significantly during the wedding season (typically October to December), major festivals such as Diwali, and auspicious days like Akshaya Tritiya (usually in April or May). These periods see heightened purchases for jewellery and gifts, often resulting in temporary surges in demand and price premiums in the domestic market. For instance, Akshaya Tritiya is traditionally considered highly auspicious for buying gold, leading to increased volumes that can exert upward pressure on local prices independent of global movements. The demand composition is predominantly jewellery-oriented, with the sector accounting for the majority of total consumption, while investment demand in bars and coins forms a smaller but variable share. Jewellery demand remains relatively stable due to cultural and social uses, whereas investment demand fluctuates more with economic sentiment , rising during periods of uncertainty or when households seek to hedge against inflation. Consumption patterns show notable differences between rural and urban areas. Rural India represents a substantial portion of demand, where gold serves as a preferred form of savings and collateral, closely linked to agricultural incomes and overall rural economic growth . Urban consumption, while significant, often includes a higher proportion of investment-oriented purchases. These regional dynamics tie domestic demand to broader economic performance, with rising incomes and economic expansion generally supporting higher gold uptake. Domestic demand factors interact with the global gold price baseline, but local seasonal and economic elements frequently amplify price movements within India. [19] [20] Currency exchange and policy impacts The domestic price of gold in India is heavily influenced by fluctuations in the INR-USD exchange rate, as global gold prices are denominated in US dollars and India is a net importer of gold. A depreciation of the rupee against the dollar increases the landed cost of gold in INR terms, pushing up domestic prices, while rupee appreciation has the opposite effect by reducing the INR-equivalent cost of imported gold. Government policies play a key role in shaping these dynamics through import-related levies and tax structures. Customs duties on gold imports add directly to the landed cost, amplifying the effect of exchange rate movements. The introduction of Goods and Services Tax (GST) in 2017 replaced earlier state-level taxes, applying a uniform rate on gold, which affects the final consumer price. [21] Historical policy adjustments, such as changes in customs duty rates to curb imports during periods of high trade deficits or currency pressure, have had notable impacts on price trends. The Reserve Bank of India's management of foreign exchange reserves and occasional interventions in the forex market can stabilize or influence the INR-USD rate , indirectly affecting gold pricing by moderating exchange rate volatility. Gold in Indian culture and economy Traditional and cultural significance Gold holds profound traditional and cultural significance in India, where it is regarded as a symbol of purity, prosperity, wealth, and auspiciousness. Unlike many metals, gold does not tarnish or corrode, reinforcing its association with eternal purity and divine favor in Indian traditions . In Hindu mythology , gold is closely linked to Goddess Lakshmi , the deity of fortune and abundance, and acquiring or wearing gold is believed to invite her blessings. Gold plays a central role in major life events, particularly weddings and festivals . During Indian weddings , gold jewellery forms an essential part of the bride's adornment, including items like the mangalsutra (sacred necklace symbolizing marital bond), bangles, necklaces, earrings, and other ornaments gifted by the family. These pieces represent security, status, and the family's love and blessings. Gold is also integral to religious ceremonies, such as offerings in temples or during rituals like naming ceremonies and housewarmings, where it signifies divine grace and good fortune. Festivals further highlight gold's cultural importance. On occasions like Diwali (festival of lights) and Akshaya Tritiya (considered highly auspicious for gold purchases), families traditionally buy or exchange gold items to invoke prosperity and ward off misfortune. These practices underscore gold's role as a harbinger of good luck and continuity. Historically, gold has served as a medium for dowry and family wealth transfer. In many communities, gold jewellery given to daughters as stridhan (woman's personal property) provided financial security and was passed down through generations, preserving family heritage and status across time. These socio-cultural practices contribute to seasonal demand spikes during wedding seasons and major festivals. Role in household savings and economy Gold plays a prominent role in Indian household savings, serving as a preferred store of value and hedge against inflation and currency depreciation. Indian households allocate a significantly larger share of their assets to physical gold compared to most other countries, with estimates indicating that gold constitutes around 6-10% of total household assets in India, in contrast to less than 1% in many developed economies where financial instruments like stocks , bonds, and bank deposits dominate. This preference for gold as a savings vehicle stems partly from cultural factors (detailed in the traditional significance section) but also from its perceived stability in a country with historically volatile financial markets and limited access to other safe investment options for many households. The substantial holdings\u2014estimated at over 25,000 tonnes across Indian households\u2014represent one of the world's largest private gold reserves, underscoring gold's macroeconomic importance in national wealth. Gold imports have a notable impact on India's balance of payments , often contributing significantly to the merchandise trade deficit and current account balance . In periods of high demand, gold has accounted for up to 10-12% of the country's total import bill, exerting pressure on the current account deficit and foreign exchange reserves; this effect has prompted periodic policy interventions such as duty adjustments to moderate inflows. During economic crises and periods of uncertainty, gold has functioned as a reliable hedge for Indian households. Demand typically surges when trust in paper assets or the rupee weakens, as seen in episodes of high inflation, financial market turmoil, or external shocks , where gold's price appreciation and liquidity provide a buffer compared to other savings forms. Gold as investment and jewellery Forms of gold purchase Gold can be purchased in India in several forms, catering to diverse needs ranging from cultural traditions and personal adornment to pure investment purposes. Physical gold is the most traditional and widely preferred option, encompassing jewellery, coins , and bars. Jewellery, often in 22-karat purity, is commonly acquired for weddings, festivals, and family heirlooms, though it typically carries making charges that increase the overall cost beyond the underlying gold price. Gold coins and bars, usually 24-karat and certified for purity , are favoured for investment as they incur minimal or no additional charges beyond a small premium, offer easier storage compared to jewellery, and can be sold back to banks or jewellers with relative ease. Physical gold provides tangibility and emotional value but involves risks of theft, storage costs, and lower liquidity in some cases. Digital gold and gold exchange-traded funds (ETFs) represent modern, paper-based alternatives. Digital gold platforms allow individuals to buy fractional quantities of 24-karat gold online, with the gold stored in insured vaults by the provider; buyers can sell it back or request physical delivery in coins or bars, offering convenience, low entry barriers, and no storage hassles. Gold ETFs, traded on stock exchanges like the NSE or BSE, track the price of physical gold and are bought/sold like shares through demat accounts; they provide high liquidity, transparency, and no physical possession risks, though they are subject to brokerage fees and market trading hours. Physical forms appeal to those valuing tangibility and cultural use, while digital and ETF options suit investors seeking liquidity, lower costs, and ease of transaction without the need for physical handling. Making charges, purity, and pricing Gold jewellery in India is predominantly sold in 22-karat purity, which contains 91.6% pure gold, rather than 24-karat (99.9% pure gold), as 24-karat gold is too soft for intricate designs and everyday wear. The Bureau of Indian Standards ( BIS ) operates a mandatory hallmarking scheme to certify purity, ensuring consumers receive gold of the declared quality. Hallmarked jewellery bears the BIS logo, the purity grade (such as \"916\" for 22 karat or \"999\" for 24 karat), the jeweller's identification mark, and the assaying centre's mark. Additionally, under the revised scheme, a unique alphanumeric code (HUID) is laser-etched on each piece for traceability. This certification helps prevent adulteration and provides assurance of purity through assaying at BIS-recognised centres . [22] Making charges represent the additional cost jewellers charge for craftsmanship, design, labour, and overheads in transforming raw gold into finished jewellery. These charges are typically applied as a percentage of the gold's base price, ranging from 8% to 25% depending on the intricacy of the design, brand reputation, and jeweller location\u2014simple designs often fall in the 10-15% range, while elaborate or branded pieces can exceed 20%. Some jewellers apply fixed making charges per gram (e.g., \u20b9200\u2013\u20b91,000 per gram) or a combination of fixed and percentage-based fees. Higher charges are common for handcrafted or lightweight pieces where labour forms a larger proportion of the cost. The final retail price of gold jewellery is calculated by adding the base gold cost (weight in grams multiplied by the current gold rate per gram, usually based on 22-karat or 24-karat rates depending on the item) to the making charges, and then applying taxes. Goods and Services Tax (GST) is levied at 3% on the total value (gold price + making charges). Some jewellers also factor in minimal wastage charges for metal loss during crafting, though this is often subsumed within making charges. The base gold rate fluctuates daily with international prices and exchange rates, making it essential to confirm the prevailing rate at the time of purchase. [23] Regulations and government schemes Import duties and taxation Gold imports into India are subject to customs duty and goods and services tax (GST). As of July 2024, the basic customs duty on gold (including bullion, coins, and jewellery ) stands at 6% ad valorem on the assessable value. This rate was reduced from 10% in the Union Budget 2024-25 , announced on July 23, 2024, to make legal imports more attractive, reduce smuggling , and ease the cost burden on consumers. A GST of 3% is levied on the supply of gold bullion, coins, and jewellery within India. This tax is applied on the transaction value, which includes the basic customs duty and any other charges for imported gold, or on the sale price for domestically sourced gold. The uniform 3% GST rate applies across these categories, unlike many other goods that face higher slabs. Historically, customs duty on gold has fluctuated significantly in response to balance of payments pressures and domestic demand. In 2012, the duty was 4%, but it was raised to 10% in 2013 amid concerns over high gold imports contributing to a widening current account deficit. The rate remained at 10% for over a decade until the 2024 reduction. These adjustments have directly influenced domestic gold prices by altering the landed cost of imports, with higher duties elevating prices and reductions helping moderate them. Sovereign Gold Bonds and official initiatives The Government of India , in consultation with the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), has launched several official initiatives to encourage investment in gold without the need to hold physical gold. These schemes provide alternatives to traditional physical gold purchases, offering benefits such as interest income, tax advantages, and reduced risks associated with storage and security. Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGBs) are the flagship scheme, introduced in 2015. They are government securities denominated in grams of gold (with a minimum investment of 1 gram and maximum limits of 4 kg for individuals/HUFs and 20 kg for trusts per financial year ). The bond price is linked to the average closing price of 999 purity gold for the preceding three business days, as published by the India Bullion and Jewellers Association Ltd (IBJA). Key features of SGBs include: Tenure : 8 years, with an exit option available on interest payment dates after the 5th year. Interest : Fixed at 2.50% per annum, payable semi-annually on the initial investment amount (the interest is taxable as income). Redemption : At maturity (or early exit), the redemption amount is based on the average closing price of 999 purity gold for the preceding three business days, ensuring investors benefit from any appreciation in gold prices. Tax benefits : Long-term capital gains tax is exempt if the bond is held till maturity. Interest income is taxed as per the investor's income tax slab. Tradability : Bonds are listed and tradable on stock exchanges (NSE and BSE), providing liquidity. SGBs can be purchased through scheduled commercial banks, designated post offices , recognized stock exchanges, and agents like Stock Holding Corporation of India Limited (SHCIL) . They are open to resident individuals, Hindu Undivided Families (HUFs), trusts, universities, and charitable institutions. Another key initiative is the Gold Monetisation Scheme (GMS) , re-launched in 2015, which allows depositors to deposit gold with banks in the form of bars, coins, or jewellery (after assaying ). In return, depositors earn interest on the gold deposited. The scheme aims to mobilize idle gold held by households and institutions, making it available for productive uses such as lending to jewellers. Deposits can be short-term (1-3 years) or medium/long-term (5-15 years), with interest rates set by banks. The principal and interest are paid in rupees equivalent to the gold value at redemption. These schemes offer several advantages over physical gold: No storage or security risks. No making charges (unlike jewellery). Interest income (unlike physical gold). Tax benefits on capital gains at maturity (for SGBs). Eligibility to use SGBs as collateral for loans. These official initiatives provide a safe, cost-effective, and transparent way to invest in gold while reducing dependence on physical holdings and supporting the domestic economy. For the most current terms, interest rates, or ongoing tranches , refer to official notifications from the Reserve Bank of India or the Government of India . References Gold Rate Today (31 January 2026), Gold Price in India - Goodreturns https://www.bankbazaar.com/gold-rate.html https://www.gold.org/goldhub/research/gold-demand-trends https://www.gold.org/goldhub/data/india-gold-demand https://www.gold.org/goldhub/research/india-gold-market-series https://www.bis.gov.in/hallmarking/ https://www.bankbazaar.com/gold-rate/gold-making-charges.html Gold, silver futures plummet 9% to hit lower circuit levels Gold, silver prices in free fall! Silver hits lower circuit at Rs 2.65 lakh in futures trade; \u2018overspeeding leads to a crash\u2019 Gold Rate Today (2 February 2026), Gold Price in India - Goodreturns Todays Gold Rate in Bangalore, 18, 22 & 24 Carat Gold Price on 2 February 2026 - Goodreturns Gold Rate Today in Bangalore - Goodreturns Bangalore Gold Rate Today Sees BIGGEST Crash In History - Goodreturns Gold Rate Today in Hyderabad - 4th February 2026 JPMorgan forecasts gold to hit $6,300 per ounce by end of 2026 UBS raises gold price target for 2026 Deutsche Bank sees gold climbing to $6000/oz in 2026 Gold & Silver Outlook 2026: Volatility, Pullbacks & Price Targets Gold Rate Today in India - LIVE Price of 24, 22 &amp; 18 Carat Gold Gold Rate Today (India) - Goodreturns Gold Rates Today - Angel One Gold Rate Today in India - LIVE Price of 24, 22 & 18 Carat Gold Gold Rates in Ghaziabad - Goodreturns Gold Rates Today Gold Rate Today Gold Rates Today Gold Rate Today Gold Rates Kerala Gold Rate per Gram - March 2025 Daily Gold Prices - March 2025 - Kerala Gold MCX India India Infoline - MCX Gold Futures (April 2026) GlobalData forecasts further upside for gold and silver in 2026 as prices beat previous estimates MCX India Official Website Gold Rate in Lucknow Today's Gold Rate in Delhi - BankBazaar Gold Price Delhi - Moneycontrol Gold Rate in Delhi Gold jumps Rs 6000, silver soars Rs 10000: How Iran\u2013US tensions affect your money Ara Eve Leo Sal 1x", "lists": [["Current gold prices", "Spot and retail rates", "City-wise variations", "Comparison with international benchmarks", "Historical trends", "Long-term price evolution", "Major periods of volatility", "Gold price outlook", "Factors influencing prices", "Global market drivers", "Domestic economic and demand factors", "Currency exchange and policy impacts", "Gold in Indian culture and economy", "Traditional and cultural significance", "Role in household savings and economy", "Gold as investment and jewellery", "Forms of gold purchase", "Making charges, purity, and pricing", "Regulations and government schemes", "Import duties and taxation", "Sovereign Gold Bonds and official initiatives", "References"], ["Tenure : 8 years, with an exit option available on interest payment dates after the 5th year.", "Interest : Fixed at 2.50% per annum, payable semi-annually on the initial investment amount (the interest is taxable as income).", "Redemption : At maturity (or early exit), the redemption amount is based on the average closing price of 999 purity gold for the preceding three business days, ensuring investors benefit from any appreciation in gold prices.", "Tax benefits : Long-term capital gains tax is exempt if the bond is held till maturity. Interest income is taxed as per the investor's income tax slab.", "Tradability : Bonds are listed and tradable on stock exchanges (NSE and BSE), providing liquidity."], ["No storage or security risks.", "No making charges (unlike jewellery).", "Interest income (unlike physical gold).", "Tax benefits on capital gains at maturity (for SGBs).", "Eligibility to use SGBs as collateral for loans."], ["Gold Rate Today (31 January 2026), Gold Price in India - Goodreturns", "https://www.bankbazaar.com/gold-rate.html", "https://www.gold.org/goldhub/research/gold-demand-trends", "https://www.gold.org/goldhub/data/india-gold-demand", "https://www.gold.org/goldhub/research/india-gold-market-series", "https://www.bis.gov.in/hallmarking/", "https://www.bankbazaar.com/gold-rate/gold-making-charges.html", "Gold, silver futures plummet 9% to hit lower circuit levels", "Gold, silver prices in free fall! Silver hits lower circuit at Rs 2.65 lakh in futures trade; \u2018overspeeding leads to a crash\u2019", "Gold Rate Today (2 February 2026), Gold Price in India - Goodreturns", "Todays Gold Rate in Bangalore, 18, 22 & 24 Carat Gold Price on 2 February 2026 - Goodreturns", "Gold Rate Today in Bangalore - Goodreturns", "Bangalore Gold Rate Today Sees BIGGEST Crash In History - Goodreturns", "Gold Rate Today in Hyderabad - 4th February 2026", "JPMorgan forecasts gold to hit $6,300 per ounce by end of 2026", "UBS raises gold price target for 2026", "Deutsche Bank sees gold climbing to $6000/oz in 2026", "Gold & Silver Outlook 2026: Volatility, Pullbacks & Price Targets", "Gold Rate Today in India - LIVE Price of 24, 22 &amp; 18 Carat Gold", "Gold Rate Today (India) - Goodreturns", "Gold Rates Today - Angel One", "Gold Rate Today in India - LIVE Price of 24, 22 & 18 Carat Gold", "Gold Rates in Ghaziabad - Goodreturns", "Gold Rates Today", "Gold Rate Today", "Gold Rates Today", "Gold Rate Today", "Gold Rates", "Kerala Gold Rate per Gram - March 2025", "Daily Gold Prices - March 2025 - Kerala Gold", "MCX India", "India Infoline - MCX Gold Futures (April 2026)", "GlobalData forecasts further upside for gold and silver in 2026 as prices beat previous estimates", "MCX India Official Website", "Gold Rate in Lucknow", "Today's Gold Rate in Delhi - BankBazaar", "Gold Price Delhi - Moneycontrol", "Gold Rate in Delhi", "Gold jumps Rs 6000, silver soars Rs 10000: How Iran\u2013US tensions affect your money"], ["Current gold prices", "Spot and retail rates", "City-wise variations", "Comparison with international benchmarks", "Historical trends", "Long-term price evolution", "Major periods of volatility", "Gold price outlook", "Factors influencing prices", "Global market drivers", "Domestic economic and demand factors", "Currency exchange and policy impacts", "Gold in Indian culture and economy", "Traditional and cultural significance", "Role in household savings and economy", "Gold as investment and jewellery", "Forms of gold purchase", "Making charges, purity, and pricing", "Regulations and government schemes", "Import duties and taxation", "Sovereign Gold Bonds and official initiatives", "References"], ["Specific beats broad \u2014 \"CRISPR\" over \"Biology\"", "People, events, and breakthroughs are ideal", "Search first to check if it already exists"], ["Select the wrong text in the article first", "Add a source link so we can verify", "One fix per submission is easiest to review"]], "tables": [], "code_blocks": [], "meta_description": "The gold price in India refers to the domestic market rate for 24-karat gold, most commonly quoted per 10 grams, serving as the primary benchmark for jewellery purchases, personal investments, and cul", "meta_keywords": "", "og_image": "https://grokipedia.com/icon-512x512.png", "js_rendered": true, "js_message": "Page appears to be rendered by a JavaScript framework; content may be incomplete.", "success": true, "error": "", "truncated": false, "fetched_bytes": 293927, "total_bytes": 293927}}